/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  Choupo STAGING record  --  PRIVATE tier (data/tmp/, gitignored).

  Aqueous solubility (solid-liquid equilibrium) of arabinose in water.

  ARITY 2: this is a SYSTEM property, not an intrinsic component field.
  Reclassified 2026-07-24 out of a comment line in
  data/tmp/components/arabinose.candidate.dat  (RECORD_SPEC.md arity rule:
  "zero usable science may live only in a comment").

  PHASE 4 (2026-07-24, agent:solubility): number DELETED on rights grounds
  (Merck Index only); solid phase NOT ESTABLISHED; the component's own
  identity unsettled (known-wrong InChIKey).

  PHASE 6 (2026-07-24, agent:solubility): the SOLID PHASE is now established
  by INFERENCE from a CC-BY crystallographic study -- beta-L-arabinopyranose,
  anhydrous -- and that study states there are no other known polymorphs or
  solvates.  An inferred phase does NOT promote a digit, and there is no
  digit here anyway: the same study measured arabinose solubility only in
  ethanol/water mixtures, never in pure water.  The InChIKey defect is
  unchanged and still upstream of everything.
  The runtime does not read this tier yet.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType      solubility;
schemaVersion   1;
recordId        arabinose-water;
participants    ( L-arabinose water );
property        aqueousSolubility;

conditions
{
    temperature   298.15 K;   // 25 C, as the withdrawn source stated
    pressure      101325 Pa;   // ambient, ASSUMED
    phase         aqueous;
    solidPhase    betaLArabinopyranose;   // anhydrous, orthorhombic P2(1)2(1)2(1)
    phaseBasis    inferredFromPhaseStability;
}

// PHASE 6 -- HOW THE PHASE WAS REASONED, AND WHY IT IS `inferred` AND NOT
// `statedBySource`.
//
// Tyson, Pask, George & Simone (Cryst. Growth Des. 22 (2022) 1371-1383,
// CC-BY 4.0) solved/refined the single-crystal structures of D-, L- and
// DL-arabinose and state, verbatim:
//
//   "l- and dl-arabinose crystals consist only of the beta-anomer of the
//    same type of ring conformation"
//   "the d- and l-arabinose crystal structures were made of only
//    six-membered ring beta-anomer molecules"
//   "Currently, there are no other known polymorphs or solvated structures
//    for either arabinose or xylose."
//
// L-arabinose: orthorhombic, space group P2(1)2(1)2(1), CCDC 2114271.
//
// That is a crystallographic landscape, not a solubility determination:
// nobody diffracted the residue of an AQUEOUS arabinose saturation
// experiment.  The three-tier rule therefore forces
// `inferredFromPhaseStability`, and an inferred phase never promotes a
// digit -- naming the only known solid does not prove the (absent)
// experimentalist had it.  The inference is nevertheless unusually safe,
// because the source asserts the ABSENCE of alternatives, not merely a
// stability ranking.
//
// A SECOND SOLID EXISTS BUT IS NOT THIS ONE.  The same work shows DL-
// arabinose forms a true RACEMIC COMPOUND (monoclinic P2(1)/c, CCDC
// 2114270) which is MORE stable than a mixture of the two enantiomers, and
// slurry experiments at 25 C convert the enantiomeric mixture into it.
// That racemic compound is a different substance from the natural single
// enantiomer named in `participants`; it is recorded here so nobody
// mistakes the two, not as an alternative phase for THIS record.
//
// MUTAROTATION applies as for the other sugars: the crystal is the beta
// anomer, the solution carries an anomeric equilibrium.
//
// THE NUMBER IS STILL GONE, AND WHY.
// Phase-2 carried ~834 g/L from The Merck Index -- paywalled, deleted in
// phase 4.  Phase 6 re-hunted: Tyson et al. 2022 measured L-arabinose
// solubility thermogravimetrically at 5-55 C but ONLY in 50:50 and 70:30
// w/w ethanol/water (their 25 C point in 50:50 ethanol/water is
// 0.1389 +/- 0.0038 g solid / g solution).  That is a different solvent and
// is deliberately NOT transplanted into this water record.  No open
// measuring primary for pure water was located.
//
// AND THE DEFECT UPSTREAM OF ALL OF THIS IS UNCHANGED: the arabinose
// COMPONENT record carries a known-wrong InChIKey (copied from xylose, per
// sugars_SOURCES.md item 7).  Until the identity is fixed there is no point
// pinning a solubility to it.  Fix the component first.
value
{
    available     none;   // still no open MEASURED digit in PURE water -- see blockedBy
}

provenance
{
    origin      -;
    citation    "WITHDRAWN in phase 4 -- the previous value came from The Merck Index (paywalled commercial compilation) and is not redistributable.  Phase 6 re-hunt found no open, readable, MEASURED replacement in PURE water";
    doi         "-";
    licence     "-";
    evidenceId  -;
    curator     "agent:solubility";
    retrieved   2026-07-24;
}

phaseProvenance
{
    citation    "Tyson, B.M., Pask, C.M., George, N. & Simone, E., Crystallization Behavior and Crystallographic Properties of dl-Arabinose and dl-Xylose Diastereomer Sugars, Cryst. Growth Des. 22 (2022) 1371-1383";
    doi         "10.1021/acs.cgd.1c01329";
    licence     "CC-BY 4.0";
    basis       "L-arabinose crystallises exclusively as the six-membered-ring beta-anomer (orthorhombic P2(1)2(1)2(1), CCDC 2114271); the work states that no other polymorph or solvated structure of arabinose is currently known.  This is a crystallographic landscape, NOT a diffraction of an aqueous saturation residue -- hence inferred, not stated";
}

adjacentSolid
{
    name        DL-arabinose;
    note        "a true racemic COMPOUND, monoclinic P2(1)/c, CCDC 2114270, MORE stable than the enantiomeric mixture (slurry conversion demonstrated at 25 C in the same work).  A different substance from the L-enantiomer this record is about -- listed so the two are never conflated";
}

nextStep        "(1) fix the component record's InChIKey -- the identity gates everything else; (2) find an open, MEASURED aqueous solubility of L-arabinose in PURE water with a stated temperature and basis";

blockedBy       "the component's InChIKey is known-wrong so the identity itself is unsettled; no open measured digit in PURE water (The Merck Index carrier deleted in phase 4; the CC-BY crystallography paper measured only ethanol/water mixtures); and the phase is INFERRED from a crystallographic landscape, which by rule cannot promote a digit even once one exists";

status          flagged;
